  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 PM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\n- Mix of Rain and Snow Showers This Afternoon/Evening\n\nNo change in thinking from the previous discussion. Radar and \nsatellite indicate ongoing lake enhanced snow showers extending \nthrough northwest and into Central Lower MI. We have seen very brief \nvisibility restrictions down to a mile or less at Houghton Lake and \nCadillac. At 1 PM, this precipitation had been very sparse in \ncoverage. However, just upstream of this, crossing northern \nWisconsin and the Michigan UP at this time, is an impressive upper\nPV lobe that was fairly well sampled on the 12Z GRB sounding. \nPrecipitation was ongoing northwest of Green Bay. Now we are \nalready seeing a substantial increase in lake enhanced precipitation\non our side of Lake Michigan and lake enhanced precipitation will\ncontinue to be the theme into early tonight.\n\nBrief snow squall conditions near the lakeshore seem probable late \nthis afternoon into early tonight. To illustrate, probabilistic post-\nprocessed bufkit guidance for Ludington (LDM) shows a greater than \n60 percent chance of snow squall indices exceeding 3 in the 5 PM to \n6 PM timeframe. Given the timing with the evening commute, will plan \nto issue an SPS for what should be a relatively small time window of \nintense precipitation combined with a burst of winds. Winds are not \nexpected to be overly strong, with gusts in the neighborhood of 30 \nmph, but this is still enough to drop visibility significantly with \nany brief intense snowfall. This relatively confined area of \nheavier precipitation and gustier winds will roll southward along \nthe lakeshore region to later affect Muskegon, Holland and South \nHaven area into early tonight. However, the more southward \nactivity should feature sub-optimal DGZ level moisture, curtailing\nprecipitation intensity somewhat. Whatever peak precipitation \nintensities do occur from Muskegon southward will happen after the\nbusiest commute period and probably have a significant liquid \ncomponent, which further reduces travel concerns.\n\nOverall, snow accumulations are not expected to be significant due \nto substantial sub-cloud riming and initially warm surface \ntemperatures. To repeat what was said previously, we could see a few \ntenths of an inch north of I-96 and perhaps up to 1 inch along US \n10, which could lead to localized slick spots on roads that \nsufficiently cool after dark tonight.\n\n\n- Rain Favored Thanksgiving into Friday, Mixing with Snow at Times\n\nNo changes of note to previous thinking, although it is worth noting \nthat there seems to be a substantial number of ensemble members \nshowing a low position over the Ohio Valley. It is possible this \njust represents part of a double lobed low. In any case, a deeper \nlow around the Ohio Valley (instead of closer to the northeast \ncoast) would still involve a lot of warm and dry lower tropospheric \nair immediately west of the precipitation shield, resulting in a \npotentially sharp cutoff of precipitation amounts as one moves from \nsoutheast Michigan northwestward. Light sloppy snow on Friday as \nnoted in the previous discussion still can't be ruled out, but \nimpacts are expected to be minimal with little to no pavement \naccumulations.\n\nQPF expectations have increased slightly with a wider spread between \nthe 25th/75th percentiles; instead of 0.25/0.75 inches, we are now \nlooking at 0.40/1.40 inches as one moves farther southeast to \nJackson. At GRR, according to NBM guidance, the spread is more like \n0.30/1.00 inches. So, this event could produce decent rainfall, but \nthere are little to no outliers in the ensemble space, indicating a \nlot less surprise potential than previously thought.\n\n\n- Chance for Weekend Rain Showers, Maybe Mixed with Snow\n\nNo changes to what was said in the previous discussion, which is \ncopied here:\n\nRenewed upper troughing over the Great Lakes this weekend looks to \nhelp produce occasional lake enhanced precipitation with W to SW low \nlevel flow over Lake Michigan. Rain showers may be mixed with some \nwet snow at times.",
